, black, white, and red-yellow-blue. Artists stripped painting down to its simplest possible parts."}},{"id":"f1557956-6ccb-4bf1-9d8a-fa9a0955fd46","createdAt":1786942185181,"transform":{"position":[0,6.900000000000011,-47.8],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"framed-painting","options":{"style":"nightsky","title":"Village Under Turning Stars","artistLine":"Post-Impressionism · 1880s","body":"Thick, visible brushstrokes that swirl. These painters cared more about feeling and movement than about copying exactly what the eye sees."}},{"id":"21554b5c-f286-4ea9-9b2f-66935330e523","createdAt":1786942185182,"transform":{"position":[14,6.900000000000011,-47.8],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"framed-painting","options":{"style":"ukiyoe","title":"Great Swell Before the Mountain","artistLine":"Ukiyo-e woodblock · 1830s","body":"Carved into wood, then printed. Flat areas of colour and bold outlines — a Japanese style that later reshaped European painting."}},{"id":"c9197c47-23a7-4b61-8b2f-99c799cad77d","createdAt":1786942185183,"transform":{"position":[-20.4,6.900000000000006,-24],"rotation":[0,1.5707963267948966,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"framed-painting","options":{"style":"pointillist","title":"Afternoon on the Riverbank","artistLine":"Pointillism · 1880s","body":"Every colour here is separate dots. Your eye blends them into new colours — the same trick a phone screen uses with red, green and blue pixels."}},{"id":"12beb36c-745f-41a3-8a50-cec3c37133a2","createdAt":1786942185185,"transform":{"position":[-20.4,6.900000000000009,-40],"rotation":[0,1.5707963267948966,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"framed-painting","options":{"style":"colorfield","title":"Three Warm Fields","artistLine":"Colour Field · 1950s","body":"No people, no places, no objects. Just enormous soft-edged rectangles of colour, meant to be stood close to and felt."}},{"id":"6f89713c-6039-41cd-980a-364db03a4767","createdAt":1786942185186,"transform":{"position":[20.4,6.9000000000000075,-32],"rotation":[0,-1.5707963267948966,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"framed-painting","options":{"style":"geometric","title":"Improvisation in Circles","artistLine":"Abstract art · 1910s","body":"The first paintings that showed nothing at all from the real world. Shape, colour and line became the subject themselves."}},{"id":"e873d191-3de0-4a40-a5c2-6afc310d4bc0","createdAt":1786942185188,"transform":{"position":[-12,12.700000000000005,-24],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f2a541"},{"id":"6e7b9bde-3e69-488e-9453-57f61c98a52c","createdAt":1786942185189,"transform":{"position":[12,12.700000000000005,-24],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f2a541"},{"id":"dd0b3062-cf17-4d8e-a4c6-03a85c734d60","createdAt":1786942185190,"transform":{"position":[-12,12.700000000000008,-42],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f2a541"},{"id":"a03e2611-4484-44ee-994c-b2b336527f9c","createdAt":1786942185192,"transform":{"position":[12,12.700000000000008,-42],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f2a541"},{"id":"525cc427-cafe-4f58-9bb0-69b4f144fa33","createdAt":1786942185193,"transform":{"position":[0,12.700000000000006,-32],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f2a541"},{"id":"5a3c6e9c-3ce7-4a4d-81c6-087e38994d29","createdAt":1786942185195,"transform":{"position":[10,1.7000000000000075,-34],"rotation":[0,-0.7,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"activity-board","options":{"number":1,"title":"Set the mobile turning","target":"Click the hanging mobile → Program.","steps":[{"cat":"control","text":"forever","depth":0},{"cat":"motion","text":"rotate 2 degrees","depth":1}],"tip":"Two degrees a frame is a slow, gallery-ish drift. Try 10 for a fairground spin, or 0.5 if you want visitors to wonder whether it is moving at all.","accent":"#6b3fa0"}},{"i